 - **After each implementation step, include exact manual test steps** for both the blank workbook and the populated workbook. See `TESTING_PLAN.md` → *Blank + populated two-track manual checks* for the canonical checklist.
 - **Regression-First Development.** Every significant change must leave the project with **equal or greater** regression coverage; coverage is never intentionally reduced. Consider whether new regression scenarios are required (bug fix / feature / architecture / schema / dashboard / financial-calculation change) and run the **Regression Discovery** checklist. See `ENGINEERING_STANDARDS.md → §12` (principle), `REGRESSION_SUITE_PLAN.md → Regression Discovery Policy + §A` (how).
+- **Minimal-first, layered validation.** Validate the changed unit with the smallest deterministic local checks first, then its focused server integration on a fixture containing only required modules, then its focused real-browser journey. Run broad/full regression suites only after those focused gates are green. A focused test must not seed unrelated financial modules merely to reuse a broad fixture. Full suites must be partitioned into bounded, resumable phases with durable progress/results and cleanup receipts; no suite may depend on one browser callback or one Apps Script invocation exceeding its safe execution budget.
+- **Autonomous completion is the default.** After an implementation or validation item starts, continue through in-scope diagnosis, fixes, redeployment to the authorized isolated target, regression reruns, cleanup, and evidence collection without waiting for another user prompt. A status question is an update request, not permission to stop, and must not end unfinished work. Stop only when the user explicitly asks, a material product/design decision or new authority is required, the same blocker meets the documented blocked threshold and cannot be resolved safely, or the item reaches its pre-commit approval checkpoint. A failed run is work to diagnose, fix, and rerun—not a handoff.
+- **Visible progress during active work.** While tools, deployments, harness runs, browser journeys, or fixes are still in progress, send the user a concise progress update at least every 60 seconds. State the current phase, the latest completed gate, and any active failure or retry. Progress reporting must not pause, replace, or terminate the underlying work.
 - **Milestone commit checkpoint.** Finish exactly one numbered roadmap item, run its focused regressions plus the full local suite, reconcile blank/fresh and representative-populated behavior, and summarize the scoped diff and runtime evidence. Then **stop** with the working tree unstaged and ask the user to approve commit/push. Do not begin implementation of the next numbered item until that checkpoint is approved and the prior item is committed and pushed. Planning or recording later work is allowed, but its runtime implementation must not overlap the active item.
 - **Detailed commit records are mandatory.** Every meaningful commit must use a concise, specific subject followed by a concrete body; a subject-only one-line commit is not acceptable. The body must record why the change was needed, the important behavior/components changed, Central-versus-bounded safety and compatibility, tests and runtime evidence, and deployment/migration/rollback impact when applicable. Documentation-only commits must still state their scope and confirm that runtime behavior, workbooks, and deployments were unaffected. Follow `COMMIT_RULES.md → Commit message format` at every approved Git checkpoint.
 - **Bounded-workbook hard stop.** Codex must never deploy to the bounded Apps Script project, run a Harness writer against the bounded or mapped-user workbook, or use that workbook for automated mutation testing. The user alone performs bounded deployment and bounded visual checks. Automated writers may target only their own continuously re-verified marked disposable workbook; isolated Central deployment still requires explicit approval.
